     receiving replies. The frequency of this polling is controlled by the normal-interval
     command. When a reply is not received, the polling instance increases the frequency at
     which it polls the device. This frequency is controlled by the critical-interval
     command, and by default, is set to send a packet every one second."

## 🔴 Measurement stance for this case (STANDING-ORDERS §7)

  STANDING-ORDERS §7 says traffic tests use scapy, not ping, because a ping cannot select
  what it sends. **T44181 is the one case in this campaign where `ping` IS the subject**
  (the queue's own rule says to say so): the frames being graded are the DUT's OWN ICMP
  echo requests, generated by its ping-poll process. They cannot be crafted by the tester,
  so they are graded where they land -- on the wire at the host NIC.

  Two independent instruments ran on every measured leg, plus a third for corroboration:
    1. `tools/hostsim.py` (NEW -- see below) on host eth3, impersonating the polled hosts
       and writing one timestamped line per echo request it receives;
    2. `tools/poll.py` sampling `show ping-poll brief` on the DUT every 2 s, i.e. the
       DEVICE's own view of the same events;
    3. the DUT's own log and `debug ping-poll` output.
  Their clocks are independent (host epoch vs DUT clock, 9 s apart), so an agreement
  between them is real evidence and not one instrument believing itself.

  **The in-leg instrument control, in every counted leg:** ping-poll instance 2 polls
  10.38.22.45, a second simulated host that is NEVER silenced. Whenever instance 1's
  traffic is expected to stop, instance 2's must still be counted; a zero for instance 1
  beside a zero for instance 2 would mean a dead capture, not a working feature.

## 🟢 NEW TOOL: tools/hostsim.py (generic, committed with this case, README entry added)

  Why it had to exist: this case needs a polled host that can be made UNREACHABLE while the
  link and the switch port stay UP. Every alternative changes the thing under test --
  `shutdown` on port1.0.10 takes the SVI down and kills the capture instrument with it,
  and dropping ICMP on the box needs an iptables rule, i.e. root on the box
  (STANDING-ORDERS §4, never done unattended).
  `tools/` had `nsresp.py` (an IPv6 Neighbor Solicitation responder) but no IPv4
  equivalent, and `dhcp4.py`'s `--arp-answer`/`--icmp-answer` only live for the length of a
  DHCP client run. `hostsim.py` is the IPv4 counterpart of `nsresp.py`: it answers ARP
  who-has and ICMP echo for any list of addresses from a fake MAC, installs nothing on the
  host, changes no host IP/route/interface, and can be silenced on a timer (`--mute-at` /
  `--unmute-at`) or by a file, for selected addresses only (`--mute-ip`), while still
  answering ARP so the DUT's neighbour entry stays valid and the loss the DUT sees is a
  pure echo-reply loss. `--log-echo` writes the per-request wire record the interval
  measurements below are read from.

  Self-test before use (15:12-15:14): with no responder the DUT's own
  `ping 10.38.22.44 repeat 4` -> "4 packets transmitted, 0 received, +3 errors, 100% packet
  loss"; with hostsim running -> "4 packets transmitted, 4 received, 0% packet loss" and
  `show arp` -> `10.38.22.44  0287.7126.162c  vlan10  port1.0.10  dynamic`. The fake MAC
  02:87:71:26:16:2c is 02:87:71 + the low three octets of 10.38.22.44, as documented.

  🔧 `tools/tb` fix needed to run it, made generically rather than worked around: the
  wrapper's `SUDO_TOOLS` allowlist was stale -- it listed 8 root tools and was missing
  `arpleg`, `ecmpflows`, `dhcp4` and `dnsresp`, all added on 2026-10-15, as well as the new
  `hostsim`. All five added. (The previous campaign never hit this because `tools/tb` did
  not work on tb277 at all then -- RISK-7, closed by group A.)

