## STEPS 2 and 9 (authored) — the trigger/script half

  Step 9 authored: "see .../ping_polling_feature_overview_guide.pdf for ping polling
                    Trigger and Script configuration"

  **The trigger half was fully measured** and is the half ping polling owns: two triggers
  of `type ping-poll 1 down` and `type ping-poll 1 up` fired at all four state changes,
  at the times tabulated above, each logged by the device with its description.

  **The script half was probed but its CONTENT was not exercised**, and here is exactly
  why. `tools/qmark.py` in trigger mode:
      awplus(config-trigger)#script  ?      <1-5>  The list position of the script
      awplus(config-trigger)#script 1  ?    FILE   The file containing the script
  and a reference to a file that does not exist is ACCEPTED with a warning, the reference
  being stored regardless:
      awplus(config-trigger)#script 1 ck44181-down.scp
      % Script /flash/ck44181-down.scp does not exist. Please ensure it is created before the trigger is activated
      awplus#show trigger 1 -> Number of scripts ... 1 /  1. ck44181-down.scp
  To run a real script the DUT needs an `.scp` file in its flash, and the only file-transfer
  path to this DUT is `copy tftp://<box>/...` from the box's `/tftproot`, which needs a
  root-level write on the box -- STANDING-ORDERS §4, **never done unattended**. Writing a
  `copy running-config flash:/x.scp` instead would hand the trigger a file of config lines
  to execute at exec level, which changes device state in an unbounded way and was refused
  on those grounds.
  ⇒ The SCRIPT-CONTENT limb is **NOT MEASURABLE on this bench unattended**. It belongs to
  the Trigger feature rather than to ping polling, and the ping-poll side of the contract
  (`type ping-poll <n> {up|down}` activating at the right moment) was measured in full.
  VERDICT steps 2/9: PASS for the trigger limb; script content NOT MEASURED, reason above.

