    awplus#show boot -> Current boot image : flash:/x960-tb277.rel (file exists)
                        Current boot config: flash:/default.cfg (file exists)
    awplus#show ping-poll brief -> header only, no instances
    awplus#show trigger -> % No triggers currently defined

  (The `(y/n)[n]` prompt was answered with `ckyn.py`'s `YN:` form; unanswered it aborts.
   `ckyn` exited 2 only because the final `show trigger` legitimately prints a `% ` line --
   `% No triggers currently defined` is the correct answer, not a failure. Gate on the
   output, never the exit code.)

  Dataplane proof, 28 s on eth3, WITH an instrument control in the same leg
  (work/s13-removed-wire2.log). Both simulated hosts were still running and answering, so
  any surviving poll would have been captured:
    tools/tb sudotool tb277 ckt1406b arpleg --tag s13-removed2 --out /tmp/ckt1406b/T44181b \
      --iface eth3 --icmp 10.38.22.34 --sender-ip 10.38.22.40 \
      --sender-mac 00:11:22:33:44:01 --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 \
      --answer 10.38.22.40=00:11:22:33:44:01 --answer-iface eth3 \
      --count 5 --gap 1 --settle 3 --wait 28 --filter "icmp or arp"

    cap s13-removed2-eth3.pcap: 12 frame(s)
      x5  ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.34  [ours]
      x5  ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.40  [ours]        <- CONTROL: the DUT answered 5/5
      x1  ARP-REQ  who-has 10.38.22.40 tell 10.38.22.34
      x1  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.40 is-at 00:11:22:33:44:01
  => The capture and the segment are provably alive (5 echo requests in, 5 replies out),
     and there is **not one frame to 10.38.22.44 or 10.38.22.45**. Removal is real in the
     dataplane, not only in the text of `show running-config`.

  ⚠️ Method note for the next run: the FIRST attempt at this control sent the same 5 echo
  requests with filter `icmp` and NO `--answer`, and got 0 replies -- not because the DUT
  was broken but because it could not ARP-resolve the crafted sender 10.38.22.40 and
  silently dropped its own replies (x960 §7.6: ARP is answered only when the sender is
  inside the target address's subnet -- it was, but nothing ANSWERED the DUT's request).
  Kept as work/s13-removed-wire.log. A control that is itself unanswered proves nothing;
  `--answer <ip>=<mac>` is required whenever the DUT must reply to a crafted source.

## Teardown verification

    tools/tb ckcon ... "show running-config" > work/post-u2.log
    python3 tools/rcdiff.py work/pre-u2.log work/post-u2.log   (exit 0 means nothing; read it)

  The diff against the pre-group capture contains **the shared underlay and nothing else**:
  `vlan database / vlan 10 name ckUNDERLAY-eth3 / vlan 48 name ckUNDERLAY-eth4 /
   vlan 10,48 state enable`, `port1.0.10 switchport access vlan 10`,
  `port1.0.48 switchport access vlan 48`, `interface vlan10 ip address 10.38.22.34/27`,
  `interface vlan48 ip address 10.38.22.66/27`. 31 added lines, every one of them underlay.
  **Zero T44181 residue**: no ping-poll, no trigger, no flash file, boot pointers back to
  `flash:/x960-tb277.rel` + `flash:/default.cfg`, autoboot still disabled.
  The underlay is left UP deliberately -- groups C and D inherit it (queue §Group B/C/D).
  File: work/s14-teardown-diff.log.

  Processes started by this case and stopped: four `hostsim.py` runs (all exited on their
  own `--duration`), three `arpleg.py` legs, one `tcpdump` via `tools/tb capture` (exited
  on its own `timeout`), one `poll.py`. Nothing of this case is still running on the box.
  No root-owned file was left in the repo; the box-side pcaps live in /tmp/ckt1406b/ and
  are not committed (logged-output.md §2).

## Findings and observations from this case

  (numbering continues the campaign: F-12 and O-12 were the highest before this case)

  **No F-level defect was found in ping polling.** Everything the authored steps claim,
  this platform does. The four items below are observations.

  🟠 O-13 — a trigger's `description` is REFUSED before its `type` is set:
     `% Please specify the trigger type first`. The authored step 2 names
     `type ping-poll <1-100> {up|down}` and `description` without an ordering constraint.
     A replay of `show running-config` is safe (it emits `type` first); a hand-written
     batch in the authored order is not, and `cfg.py` will stop and abandon the rest of it.
     Evidence: work/s5-s6-config.log line 76.

  🟠 O-14 — the ping-poll PERIOD is `timeout + interval`, not `interval`. With
     `timeout 2`, `normal-interval 5`, `critical-interval 2` the measured period was
     **5.0-5.2 s while Up** (reply arrives in ~1 ms, then the normal interval) and
     **3.7-4.6 s, mean 4.14 s, while Down** (= timeout 2 + critical-interval 2), over 36
     gaps on the host's own packet record and confirmed by the DUT's own debug lines at
     02:31:38/42/46/50/54 with "unsuccessful" exactly 2 s after each send.
     This matters because the authored precondition says the critical interval is "set to
     send a packet every one second" by default -- with the default `timeout 1` that is an
     actual 2 s period, and a tester who configures `critical-interval 2` and measures 4 s
     may wrongly grade the command broken. NOT a defect: the behaviour is self-consistent
     and is the only sane way to schedule a poll that must first time out.

  🟠 O-15 — ping polling writes NO log line of its own for a state change.
     `show log | include PING` returned nothing across four measured transitions. The only
     log record is `TRIGGER[...]: Trigger N (...) activated`, and that exists only if a
     trigger of type ping-poll has been configured. A deployment that uses ping polling
     purely to drive its own state (no trigger) leaves no audit trail of a transition.
     Evidence: work/s4-dutlog.log.

  🟠 O-16 — there is no `show debugging ping-poll` (`% Invalid input detected`) and the
     full `show debugging` output has no ping-poll section. The debug state is readable
     only from the `Debugging` field of `show ping-poll <n>`.
     Evidence: work/s11-debug.log, work/s9-script-file.log.

  Two tool/method notes, fixed generically rather than worked around (all committed):
   - `tools/tb`'s `SUDO_TOOLS` allowlist was missing `arpleg`, `ecmpflows`, `dhcp4`,
     `dnsresp` (all added 2026-10-15) and the new `hostsim`. All five added.
   - `arpleg.py --out DIR` created a ROOT-OWNED directory, so a later non-root tool could
     not write into it -- `poll.py` completed its 180 s of sampling and then died with
     `PermissionError: '/tmp/ckt1406b/T44181/poll.done'`. No measurement was lost, but the
     donefile convention silently breaks. `arpleg.py` now chowns its `--out` directory back
     to `SUDO_UID`, as it already did for the pcaps.
   - `console.ADVISORY` gained `does not exist\. Please ensure it is created` -- the
     trigger `script N <file>` warning is an ADVISORY, not a rejection: the reference IS
     stored (`show trigger 1 -> Number of scripts 1`). Added with the transcript the
     console.py rule requires (work/s9-script-file.log).

## Run quality

  CLEAN. No confounder. Every leg had an in-leg instrument control; every transition was
  read from two instruments with independent clocks; baseline-off was measured before any
  "it works" claim; removal and reboot persistence were both proven in the dataplane and
  not only in `show running-config`. The one limb not exercised (the CONTENT of a trigger
  script) is named above with its reason, is bench-level rather than platform-level, and
  belongs to the Trigger feature rather than to ping polling.

VERDICT: PASS -- all 11 authored steps exercised (step 1's ART run substituted by a direct
measurement of the limit it measures: 100 instances accepted, 0 and 101 rejected), plus
baseline-off, wire-measured normal and critical intervals, fail-count/up-count transitions
corroborated by the DUT's own trigger log to within 1 s, per-instance `clear`, removal and
reboot persistence both proven in the dataplane. No platform defect; four observations
(O-13..O-16). Trigger-script CONTENT not measurable unattended (needs a root-level write to
the box's /tftproot -- STANDING-ORDERS §4).
